# =============================================================================
#  CqrsApiExample: a guided tour of the API
# =============================================================================
#  Requires the VS Code "REST Client" extension (humao.rest-client).
#  Click "Send Request" above each request, top to bottom: some requests reuse
#  values from earlier ones (marked "needs: <name>").
#
#  Start the API first, either way works on the same URL:
#    docker compose up --build                    (SQL Server + API in Docker)
#    docker compose up db  +  dotnet run --project src/Api
#
#  Every error is a ProblemDetails (RFC 9457) body with
#  Content-Type: application/problem+json, a "traceId" to find it in the logs,
#  and "errorCodes" with stable codes in the Area.Name form (e.g. User.NotFound).
#  Clients should branch on errorCodes, not on the human-readable messages.
#
#  Emails are generated with {{$guid}}, so the file can be run any number of
#  times without clashing with data from earlier runs.
# =============================================================================

@baseUrl = http://localhost:5235
@api = {{baseUrl}}/api


###
# =============================================================================
#  1. Discovery and login
# =============================================================================

### 1.1 OpenAPI document: every endpoint with its possible responses
#   200, describes GET/POST /api/Users and GET /api/Users/{id}, including the
#   error statuses each one can return (400, 404, 409, 500).
#   Swagger UI (open in a browser): http://localhost:5235/swagger
#   Both exist only in the Development environment.
GET {{baseUrl}}/openapi/v1.json

### 1.2 Log in as the seed user (Development only; password in DbInitializer.SeedUserPassword)
#   200 { accessToken, tokenType: "Bearer", expiresIn: 1800 }, with Cache-Control: no-store.
#   Every GET below sends this token (needs: login). Without it they answer
#   401 Request.Unauthorized (see 7.4). Registering (3.1) and logging in are anonymous.
# @name login
POST {{api}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "email": "jane.doe@example.com",
    "password": "JaneDoe.Dev.2026"
}


###
# =============================================================================
#  2. Reading users (CQRS read side: projections straight to a read model)
# =============================================================================

### 2.1 List users
#   200, ordered by creation date. The seed user (Jane Doe) is created at
#   startup in Development.
# @name listUsers
GET {{api}}/users
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.accessToken}}

### 2.2 Get one user by id (needs: listUsers)
#   200 with the same fields as in the list.
GET {{api}}/users/{{listUsers.response.body.$[0].id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.accessToken}}


###
# =============================================================================
#  3. Creating a user (CQRS write side: domain rules, unit of work, events)
# =============================================================================

### 3.1 Create a user
#   201 Created, with:
#   - a Location header pointing to the new user (used in 3.2);
#   - normalized data: name/surname trimmed, email trimmed and lower-cased
#     (see "name" and "email" in the response);
#   - a welcome email, sent through the transactional outbox: the event is
#     saved with the user in one transaction and a background worker sends it
#     about 2 s later (retried if the email service fails; see 8.4).
#     The email sender is a stand-in that only logs it, so look for
#     `Email "Welcome!" sent` in the API output (docker compose logs api).
# @name createUser
POST {{api}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "name": "  Ada  ",
    "surname": "Lovelace",
    "email": "  Ada.{{$guid}}@Example.COM  ",
    "password": "correct horse battery staple"
}

### 3.2 Follow the Location header of the new user (needs: createUser)
#   200, the user as stored.
GET {{createUser.response.headers.Location}}
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.accessToken}}


###
# =============================================================================
#  4. Business errors
# =============================================================================

### 4.1 Duplicate email: 409 User.DuplicateEmail (needs: createUser)
#   Sends exactly the email typed in 3.1 (mixed case, with spaces). Emails are
#   compared after normalization, so it is still a duplicate.
#   A unique index in the database also covers concurrent requests (see 6.2).
#   No welcome email is sent for a rejected user: its event was never stored.
POST {{api}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "name": "Ada",
    "surname": "Again",
    "email": "{{createUser.request.body.$.email}}",
    "password": "correct horse battery staple"
}

### 4.2 Wrong password: 401 Auth.InvalidCredentials (needs: createUser)
#   An unknown email gets exactly the same answer (try any other email), so
#   the API never tells which emails have an account.
POST {{api}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "email": "{{createUser.request.body.$.email}}",
    "password": "not the password"
}

### 4.3 Log in as the new user (needs: createUser)
#   The email is matched after normalization, like the duplicate check in 4.1.
POST {{api}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "email": "{{createUser.request.body.$.email}}",
    "password": "correct horse battery staple"
}

### 4.4 User not found: 404 User.NotFound
GET {{api}}/users/bc0b9fa8-cfd3-430e-adf8-7afbed42a5a1
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.accessToken}}


###
# =============================================================================
#  5. Input errors (400)
# =============================================================================
#  Validation errors come as "errors" grouped by FIELD (for forms) plus
#  "errorCodes" (for code). Each field reports only its first failing rule.
#  The codes are the same whether the request validator or the domain entity
#  rejects the value.

### 5.1 Several invalid fields at once
#   400 errorCodes ["User.InvalidName", "User.InvalidEmail"]
#   errors: Name -> "'Name' must not be empty."
#           Email -> "'Email' is not a valid email address."
POST {{api}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "name": "",
    "surname": "Lovelace",
    "email": "not-an-email",
    "password": "correct horse battery staple"
}

### 5.2 Every field invalid
#   400 errorCodes ["User.InvalidName", "User.InvalidSurname", "User.InvalidEmail"]
POST {{api}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "name": "",
    "surname": "",
    "email": "",
    "password": "correct horse battery staple"
}

### 5.3 Whitespace only counts as empty
#   400 errorCodes ["User.InvalidName"]
POST {{api}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "name": "   ",
    "surname": "Lovelace",
    "email": "ada.{{$guid}}@example.com",
    "password": "correct horse battery staple"
}

### 5.4 Too long: the limits come from the domain (User.NameMaxLength = 100)
#   400 errorCodes ["User.InvalidName"]
#   "The length of 'Name' must be 100 characters or fewer. You entered 101 characters."
POST {{api}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "name": "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa",
    "surname": "Lovelace",
    "email": "ada.{{$guid}}@example.com",
    "password": "correct horse battery staple"
}

### 5.5 Missing field: same error as an empty one
#   400 errorCodes ["User.InvalidSurname"]
POST {{api}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "name": "Ada",
    "email": "ada.{{$guid}}@example.com",
    "password": "correct horse battery staple"
}

### 5.6 Explicit null: same error as an empty one
#   400 errorCodes ["User.InvalidSurname"]
POST {{api}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "name": "Ada",
    "surname": null,
    "email": "ada.{{$guid}}@example.com",
    "password": "correct horse battery staple"
}

### 5.7 Empty id: 400 User.InvalidId (a GUID, but not a valid user id)
GET {{api}}/users/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.accessToken}}


###
# =============================================================================
#  6. Unreadable requests
# =============================================================================
#  Requests MVC cannot even bind never reach validation. They get the same
#  shape with errorCodes ["Request.Invalid"]; "errors" holds the JSON path of
#  the problem with a generic message. The parser's own message is not passed
#  on, because it can name internal types.

### 6.1 Malformed JSON: 400 Request.Invalid
#   errors: "$.name" -> "The input was not valid."
POST {{api}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Ada",

### 6.2 Wrong JSON type (number instead of text): 400 Request.Invalid
#   errors: "$.name" -> "The input was not valid." (no internal type names)
POST {{api}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "name": 123,
    "surname": "Lovelace",
    "email": "ada.{{$guid}}@example.com",
    "password": "correct horse battery staple"
}

### 6.3 Empty body: 400 Request.Invalid ("A non-empty request body is required.")
POST {{api}}/users
Content-Type: application/json


###
# =============================================================================
#  7. HTTP-level errors (raised by ASP.NET Core before the API code runs)
# =============================================================================
#  They still come as ProblemDetails with a "Request.*" error code, so a client
#  can handle every error the same way.

### 7.1 Id that is not a GUID: 404 Request.NotFound
#   The route only matches GUIDs ({id:guid}), so no endpoint is found.
#   Compare with 4.4: Request.NotFound = no such route,
#                     User.NotFound    = the route exists, the user doesn't.
GET {{api}}/users/not-a-guid
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.accessToken}}

### 7.2 Wrong Content-Type: 415 Request.UnsupportedMediaType
POST {{api}}/users
Content-Type: text/plain

Ada Lovelace

### 7.3 Method not supported: 405 Request.MethodNotAllowed
#   The "Allow" response header lists what is supported (GET).
#   With a token: without one, every endpoint that isn't anonymous (including an
#   unknown route or method) answers 401 first, so nothing is revealed to anonymous callers.
DELETE {{api}}/users/{{listUsers.response.body.$[0].id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.accessToken}}

### 7.4 No token: 401 Request.Unauthorized
#   The WWW-Authenticate header says "Bearer". Same answer for an expired or
#   tampered token, with error="invalid_token" in that header.
GET {{api}}/users

### 7.5 Invalid token: 401 Request.Unauthorized
GET {{api}}/users
Authorization: Bearer not.a.token


###
# =============================================================================
#  8. Behaviours that need more than one request
# =============================================================================
#
#  8.1 Unexpected error: 500 General.Unexpected
#      Stop the database and list the users again (request 2.1):
#        docker compose stop db
#      Database calls are retried on transient failures (3 retries, short
#      delays), so after about 20 s the API gives up and answers:
#        500, errorCodes ["General.Unexpected"], "detail" with the exception
#        message ("The maximum number of retries (3) was exceeded...";
#        Development only, hidden in other environments).
#      The full exception is logged once, with its traceId. Bring the db back:
#        docker compose start db
#
#  8.2 Concurrent duplicates: exactly one 201, the rest 409 (never 500)
#      Ten simultaneous requests with the same email, from a terminal:
#        email="race-$RANDOM$RANDOM@example.com"; for i in $(seq 10); do \
#          curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} ' -X POST http://localhost:5235/api/users \
#          -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
#          -d "{\"name\":\"Race\",\"surname\":\"Test\",\"email\":\"$email\"}" & done; wait; echo
#      The check in code can't stop two requests that arrive together; the
#      unique index in the database does, and the API turns that into a 409.
#      Only one user is stored and only one welcome email is sent.
#
#  8.3 Logs (docker compose logs api, or the dotnet run output)
#      Each request is logged with its elapsed time, and failures with their
#      error codes, for example:
#        CreateUserCommand handled in 42.9 ms
#        CreateUserCommand failed in 2.5 ms with User.DuplicateEmail
#      Request bodies are never logged (they carry personal data).
#
#  8.4 Outbox (how the welcome email of 3.1 is delivered)
#      Every domain event is stored in OutboxMessages in the same transaction
#      as the change, then handled in the background. Watch it in VS Code
#      (SQLTools connection "CqrsApiExample") after running 3.1:
#        SELECT Type, Attempts, OccurredAt, ProcessedAt, Error
#        FROM OutboxMessages ORDER BY OccurredAt DESC;
#        SELECT * FROM OutboxMessageConsumers;   -- which handler finished which message
#      A failing handler is retried with backoff (5 s, 10 s, ... up to 10 min,
#      10 attempts); after that the row keeps its Error for diagnosis. Handlers
#      that already succeeded are never run again for the same message.
#      If the API stops before sending, the message is still there and is sent
#      when the API starts again.
